      "updated": 1793750000000,
      "created": 1793750000000,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What this chart is about\n- This is a separate version of the capstone. Here Northbridge does not run an ERP like Ledgerline. It runs NBAS, an old in-house purchase and accounts system built in\n  2009: orders, goods receipts, invoices and payments sit in one SQL Server database on Northbridge's own server.\n- NBAS has no modern API, only an old XML interface (send it an XML message, it posts or returns a record), and that interface has no login of its own. It has no holds, no\n  approval records, no payment runs and one shared login per department, so it cannot show who entered, checked or approved anything. The rules live in the finance manual\n  and in people's heads.\n- The story is the same: Northbridge Cycles (Pune, Maharashtra) ordered 18,000 battery packs from Voltway Cells (Gujarat) at Rs.1,276.80 each (order PO/2026/01120). Both\n  are Indian residents; GST is 18%.\n- The contract says: the price on the delivery date applies; the cash-discount tiers (0.25% within 15 days, 0.125% within 30) count from the delivery date and reduce the\n  GST too, but a discount agreed after the supply cannot reduce GST (CGST Act s.15(3)(b)); defective goods must be claimed from Voltway within 2 days; goods broken in\n  transit are Northbridge's risk, claimed from its insurer.\n- Voltway's price became Rs.1,332.80 on 1st October. The packs arrived on 2nd October; 300 were broken in transit. Invoice 4471 bills 18,000 at Rs.1,332.80. It is due on\n  16th November; Northbridge would normally pay it on Friday 20th November.\n- On 7th October Voltway emailed better terms (0.5% if paid within 15 days of delivery, 0.25% within 30) and called Emma the next day. Voltway never learns about\n  Northbridge's internal steps. This invoice takes Voltway's purchases this year past Rs.50 lakh, so TDS starts (Income-tax Act 2025, s.393(1), formerly 194Q).\n- Frame 1 (the top band): today, by hand on NBAS, with red notes where it goes wrong. Frame 2 (the band under it, same phases): we wrap NBAS's XML interface in a small\n  adapter of our own (our API), keep a read-only database copy for heavy reading, and build an approval layer (the gateway) that enforces the rules, records every approval\n  in each person's own login, and is the only thing that writes into NBAS. The logic is in code; rates, limits and people are in approved tables, so they change without\n  new code.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": "intro",
      "originalText": "What this chart is about\n- This is a separate version of the capstone. Here Northbridge does not run an ERP like Ledgerline. It runs NBAS, an old in-house purchase and accounts system built in\n  2009: orders, goods receipts, invoices and payments sit in one SQL Server database on Northbridge's own server.\n- NBAS has no modern API, only an old XML interface (send it an XML message, it posts or returns a record), and that interface has no login of its own. It has no holds, no\n  approval records, no payment runs and one shared login per department, so it cannot show who entered, checked or approved anything. The rules live in the finance manual\n  and in people's heads.\n- The story is the same: Northbridge Cycles (Pune, Maharashtra) ordered 18,000 battery packs from Voltway Cells (Gujarat) at Rs.1,276.80 each (order PO/2026/01120). Both\n  are Indian residents; GST is 18%.\n- The contract says: the price on the delivery date applies; the cash-discount tiers (0.25% within 15 days, 0.125% within 30) count from the delivery date and reduce the\n  GST too, but a discount agreed after the supply cannot reduce GST (CGST Act s.15(3)(b)); defective goods must be claimed from Voltway within 2 days; goods broken in\n  transit are Northbridge's risk, claimed from its insurer.\n- Voltway's price became Rs.1,332.80 on 1st October. The packs arrived on 2nd October; 300 were broken in transit. Invoice 4471 bills 18,000 at Rs.1,332.80. It is due on\n  16th November; Northbridge would normally pay it on Friday 20th November.\n- On 7th October Voltway emailed better terms (0.5% if paid within 15 days of delivery, 0.25% within 30) and called Emma the next day. Voltway never learns about\n  Northbridge's internal steps. This invoice takes Voltway's purchases this year past Rs.50 lakh, so TDS starts (Income-tax Act 2025, s.393(1), formerly 194Q).\n- Frame 1 (the top band): today, by hand on NBAS, with red notes where it goes wrong. Frame 2 (the band under it, same phases): we wrap NBAS's XML interface in a small\n  adapter of our own (our API), keep a read-only database copy for heavy reading, and build an approval layer (the gateway) that enforces the rules, records every approval\n  in each person's own login, and is the only thing that writes into NBAS. The logic is in code; rates, limits and people are in approved tables, so they change without\n  new code.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "key",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 610,
      "width": 2286.6,
      "height": 75.0,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#868e96",
